## Proof

Let $\operatorname{Log} w$ denote the **principal branch** of the logarithm, defined by
$$\operatorname{Log} w = \ln|w| + i\,\operatorname{Arg} w, \qquad \operatorname{Arg} w \in (-\pi, \pi).$$
It is a standard fact that $\operatorname{Log} w$ is analytic on $\mathbb{C}\setminus(-\infty,0]$, with branch cut along the non‑positive real axis $(-\infty,0]$.

Consider the candidate branch
$$f(z) = \operatorname{Log}(2z-1).$$

The map $g(z)=2z-1$ is entire (a non‑constant affine map), hence analytic everywhere. The composition $\operatorname{Log}(2z-1)=\operatorname{Log}\circ\, g$ is analytic at every point $z$ where $g(z)=2z-1$ lies in the domain of analyticity of $\operatorname{Log}$, i.e., where
$$2z-1 \notin (-\infty,0].$$

It remains to identify precisely the set of $z$ for which $2z-1\in(-\infty,0]$.

Write $z=x+iy$. Then
$$2z-1 = (2x-1)+i(2y).$$
This belongs to $(-\infty,0]$ if and only if it is real and non‑positive, i.e.,
$$2y=0 \quad\text{and}\quad 2x-1\le 0,$$
which is equivalent to
$$y=0 \quad\text{and}\quad x\le \tfrac{1}{2}.$$

Thus
$$\{z\in\mathbb{C}: 2z-1\in(-\infty,0]\} = \{x+iy : x\le \tfrac{1}{2},\; y=0\},$$
which is exactly the specified ray.

Therefore $f(z)=\operatorname{Log}(2z-1)$ is analytic on
$$\mathbb{C}\setminus\{x+iy : x\le \tfrac{1}{2},\; y=0\},$$
i.e., everywhere in the complex plane except on the ray $\{x+iy:x\le \tfrac{1}{2},\,y=0\}$.

This gives the desired branch of $\log(2z-1)$. $\blacksquare$

